                            <par_12
                                jcr:primaryType="nt:unstructured"
                                sling:resourceType="wcm/foundation/components/parsys">
                                <text_copy
                                    cq:styleIds="[1659545825678]"
                                    jcr:created="{Date}2022-12-10T17:19:15.327-05:00"
                                    jcr:createdBy="chiucx"
                                    jcr:lastModified="{Date}2025-12-16T12:19:50.184-05:00"
                                    jcr:lastModifiedBy="VALENSX5"
                                    jcr:primaryType="nt:unstructured"
                                    sling:resourceType="abbvie-com2/components/text/v2/text"
                                    text="&lt;p>After preliminary discussions and some preparation, we were subjected to a thorough and detailed assessment by members of the board of the Swiss Headache Society in November 2025. For over 30 years, the Swiss Headache Society (SKG) has been uniting specialists, primary care physicians and scientists dealing with the subject of headaches and migraines.&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;&lt;p>Migraines affect around 15 per cent of the working population. Creating a working environment in which migraine patients can develop their full potential and feel comfortable is therefore of great interest to AbbVie – on the one hand, because the well-being of our employees is central for us, and on the other, because it helps to reduce absenteeism at work and also counteracts so-called &amp;quot;presenteeism&amp;quot; (reduced work performance due to illness).&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;&lt;p>The &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.headache.ch/migraine-friendly/migraine-friendly-workspace&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;>Label of the Swiss Headache Society&lt;/a> recognises companies that take an open approach to the condition and, through this acceptance and targeted measures, relieve and support those affected, thereby also reducing the stigma that migraine sufferers continue to suffer from greatly. This is because migraine is still far too often misjudged as a psychosomatic rather than a neurological illness.&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;"
                                    textIsRich="true"/>
                            </par_12>
